WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.033
-Great to see you, bud.
You look great.

00:00:03.033 --> 00:00:04.334
Thank you for being here.

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:06.367
And thanks for being on this
coast and doing our show.

00:00:06.367 --> 00:00:08.601
I appreciate it because
you're over in San Francisco.

00:00:08.601 --> 00:00:10.601
-Exactly. Yeah.
-But you were telling me

00:00:10.601 --> 00:00:13.200
that you've been testing out
these driverless cars.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.334
-Yes, yes.
I am a beta tester

00:00:16.334 --> 00:00:19.234
for Waymo,
which is Google's car.

00:00:19.234 --> 00:00:21.901
You know, a lot of the big
companies have driverless cars,

00:00:21.901 --> 00:00:23.234
and they're all over the city.

00:00:23.234 --> 00:00:25.400
I mean, they're driving 24/7,

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.501
and you can just order them
like an Uber.

00:00:28.501 --> 00:00:30.534
-So what is it shaped like?

00:00:30.534 --> 00:00:33.000
-It's like a little
sort of electric Jaguar,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.601
and it's got cameras on it
and all these sensors.

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:37.901
And you have an app
and you order it like an Uber,

00:00:37.901 --> 00:00:40.968
and it just pulls up,
and you can --

00:00:40.968 --> 00:00:43.100
-There's no driver?
-There's no driver.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.834
Your app unlocks the door,
you get in, and you go.

00:00:46.834 --> 00:00:49.067
And I've done it
probably a dozen times

00:00:49.067 --> 00:00:50.367
and just did it yesterday.

00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:51.400
-Wow.
-Yeah.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:52.634
-And you're not scared?

00:00:52.634 --> 00:00:54.501
-You know, it's a little weird
at the beginning.

00:00:54.501 --> 00:00:56.400
I mean, you --
The first time I got in,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.801
you're sort of thinking, like,

00:00:57.801 --> 00:00:59.601
the scene
in "The Fast and Furious."

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:01.367
You know,
when the cars are, like,

00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:03.033
dropping out
of the parking lot

00:01:03.033 --> 00:01:05.834
and, you know, like, somebody
remotely controlling it.

00:01:05.834 --> 00:01:08.701
But, you know, after a while,
it's so normal.

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:11.133
I was in one with my son
the other day,

00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:12.734
and we were whispering

00:01:12.734 --> 00:01:14.734
because we didn't want
the driver to hear us.

00:01:14.734 --> 00:01:16.067
-Yeah.
-But then you realize

00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:18.200
there's no driver.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:19.534
And they're absolutely amazing.

00:01:19.534 --> 00:01:22.200
I mean, there are still
some kinks to work out,

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.467
but these are gonna be
all over --

00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:27.868
-There's some kinks.
-A few.

00:01:27.868 --> 00:01:30.601
But, I mean, they've driven
millions of miles

00:01:30.601 --> 00:01:32.133
all over San Francisco.

00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:33.534
-Will be in every city,
you think?

00:01:33.534 --> 00:01:34.901
-I think so...

00:01:34.901 --> 00:01:36.667
And people are super excited.

00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:38.834
I mean, people get really
kind of freaked out about it.

00:01:38.834 --> 00:01:41.167
Like, when we --
when I get out of it,

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:44.367
people gather around
like paparazzi and snap pictures

00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:45.934
because they're so excited

00:01:45.934 --> 00:01:48.501
to see something coming
out of a driverless car.

00:01:48.501 --> 00:01:51.534
-You actually brought us a video
of a ride you took in one.

00:01:51.534 --> 00:01:52.701
Thank you for doing this.

00:01:52.701 --> 00:01:53.968
Let's go through
what's going on.

00:01:53.968 --> 00:01:57.033
Wait. So why even have
the wheel, though?

00:01:57.033 --> 00:01:59.167
-Yeah, I know, I know.
-That scares me a little bit.

00:01:59.167 --> 00:02:02.100
I don't want to see that.
Oh, no. This is terrible.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.100
-This is it.
I'm in the back seat. Yeah.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.133
-No, no no, no, no, no,
-Yeah. All -- Yeah. All over.

00:02:09.133 --> 00:02:10.767
And you can take it
all over the city

00:02:10.767 --> 00:02:12.234
anywhere in San Francisco.
-No.

00:02:12.234 --> 00:02:13.868
See, if I was designing
this thing --

00:02:13.868 --> 00:02:15.467
I'll talk to Google.
We'll figure it out.

00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:18.200
If I was designing that, I would
get rid of the whole wheel.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.567
Get rid of the passenger seat.
I don't want to see the seat.

00:02:20.567 --> 00:02:22.167
-No. Right. Yeah.
-What does that do for me?

00:02:22.167 --> 00:02:23.834
-That's right.
See a wheel? That's frightening.

00:02:23.834 --> 00:02:25.567
-Exactly.
-I just want to be in --

00:02:25.567 --> 00:02:27.067
-You just want the thing. Right?

00:02:27.067 --> 00:02:29.534
-I want to be in, like,
the Las Vegas Sphere almost.

00:02:29.534 --> 00:02:31.334
I don't even want
to look out the window.

00:02:31.334 --> 00:02:33.133
And I want to be in Paris.
-That's coming.

00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:34.601
-That is, right?
-That's coming.

00:02:34.601 --> 00:02:37.567
-This interview will probably --
In 20 years, people will laugh

00:02:37.567 --> 00:02:39.901
and they'll go, "Oh, Jimmy
Fallon said this would never..."

00:02:39.901 --> 00:02:41.734
I am saying
it will be the future.

00:02:41.734 --> 00:02:43.234
-It is.
-It is the future.

00:02:43.234 --> 00:02:44.734
-In 10 years.
-It's happening.

00:02:44.734 --> 00:02:47.501
-This -- What you just saw there
will be everywhere.

00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:49.801
-I agree with you, but I don't
think the wheel will be there.

00:02:49.801 --> 00:02:51.868
-No. Yeah.
I think it's a good idea.

00:02:51.868 --> 00:02:54.901
-I think the wheel is a waste
of space, and it freaks me out.

00:02:54.901 --> 00:02:56.601
I don't even need
to see the front.

00:02:56.601 --> 00:02:58.868
Do you want to see the street?
-No, you don't need to see it.

00:02:58.868 --> 00:03:00.300
-I would get scared.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:01.868
-You can actually have just
a movie in the windshield.

00:03:01.868 --> 00:03:03.701
-I don't even want to --
Because I'd be like,

00:03:03.701 --> 00:03:05.934
"Aah! Watch out!"
-Yeah.

00:03:05.934 --> 00:03:07.734
-Can you --
Can you make suggestions?

00:03:07.734 --> 00:03:08.868
-You can't. No.

00:03:08.868 --> 00:03:11.801
-That's gonna be a problem
for New Yorkers.

00:03:11.801 --> 00:03:13.267
-You can't.
-"Make a left on Madison!

00:03:13.267 --> 00:03:15.033
What's wrong with you?!"
Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:15.033 --> 00:03:17.667
That's gonna be -- New York's
gonna have a different thing.

00:03:17.667 --> 00:03:20.234
-Yeah.
-You have a lot of jobs.

00:03:20.234 --> 00:03:22.601
You do a great job
with all of your jobs.

00:03:22.601 --> 00:03:24.267
And I was going --
what do you do --

00:03:24.267 --> 00:03:25.567
Like, when you're not working,

00:03:25.567 --> 00:03:27.634
what do you do
to enjoy yourself?

00:03:27.634 --> 00:03:29.667
-You know, one of the things
I do to kind of de-stress --

00:03:29.667 --> 00:03:31.968
because, you know,
we all have stressful jobs --

00:03:31.968 --> 00:03:34.467
every couple of months
I go and work

00:03:34.467 --> 00:03:36.901
in some of
the most stressful places,

00:03:36.901 --> 00:03:39.801
which are kitchens
of fine-dining restaurants.

00:03:39.801 --> 00:03:41.868
And I'll go work there
for like 3 or 4 days,

00:03:41.868 --> 00:03:44.100
and I'll do prep.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:45.968
You know,
I'll peel tomatoes for hours.

00:03:45.968 --> 00:03:48.300
I just did this at a restaurant
called Harbor House

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:49.734
in Northern California.

00:03:49.734 --> 00:03:52.334
I've done it at a restaurant
called SingleThread.

00:03:52.334 --> 00:03:55.167
-This is --
You just go and prep food?

00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:57.033
-For hours.
And just peel tomatoes.

00:03:57.033 --> 00:03:58.267
This is kind of a zen...

00:03:58.267 --> 00:04:00.367
-And what happens
when you peel the tomatoes?

00:04:00.367 --> 00:04:01.601
They just go into a soup?

00:04:01.601 --> 00:04:04.000
-It's sliced in half,
and it's a little tomato

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.534
on a plate of other things.

00:04:05.534 --> 00:04:07.968
Like, people who eat
the food wouldn't know

00:04:07.968 --> 00:04:09.701
that somebody spent an hour,

00:04:09.701 --> 00:04:13.467
you know, stuffing
shishito peppers or...

00:04:13.467 --> 00:04:15.534
-So it gives you a little
appreciation for those things.

00:04:15.534 --> 00:04:17.234
You go, "Hey.
Someone did this for an hour."

00:04:17.234 --> 00:04:18.467
-And it's really hard work.

00:04:18.467 --> 00:04:20.367
But there's something
very centering about it

00:04:20.367 --> 00:04:22.467
because it's this
repetitive job,

00:04:22.467 --> 00:04:24.434
and it makes me feel like

00:04:24.434 --> 00:04:27.300
I'm stepping out of my world
for a few days.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.868
-These are Michelin Star chefs.

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:30.367
-These are Michelin Star chefs.
Yeah.

00:04:30.367 --> 00:04:32.934
-Have you ever cooked
for a Michelin Star chef?

00:04:32.934 --> 00:04:34.868
-I have, yeah. I have.

00:04:34.868 --> 00:04:36.801
I've cooked
for our friend Daniel Humm.

00:04:36.801 --> 00:04:38.701
-Daniel Humm. I love him.
He's --

00:04:38.701 --> 00:04:40.634
-He's amazing.
-You cooked for Daniel Humm?

00:04:40.634 --> 00:04:42.734
-I made him a sandwich.
It was a sandwich.

00:04:42.734 --> 00:04:44.868
-That counts. That counts.
-It counts.

00:04:44.868 --> 00:04:46.534
-I would be --
-it counts.

00:04:46.534 --> 00:04:48.501
-Questlove knows Daniel, too.
Yeah.

00:04:48.501 --> 00:04:50.834
Wait. But what kind of sandwich?

00:04:50.834 --> 00:04:52.934
-It was a veggie sandwich.
You know.

00:04:52.934 --> 00:04:54.167
-Peanut butter and jelly?

00:04:54.167 --> 00:04:56.200
-Just a bunch of veggie stuff.
-Veggies?

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:58.701
-And what'd he think?
-He thought it was pretty good.

00:04:58.701 --> 00:05:00.167
Yeah. I've -- You know --

00:05:00.167 --> 00:05:03.234
And the thing about cooking
for for Michelin Star chefs

00:05:03.234 --> 00:05:06.234
is that people get nervous
to cook for them, right?

00:05:06.234 --> 00:05:07.367
Because, I mean, you're cooking

00:05:07.367 --> 00:05:08.868
for the greatest chefs
in the world --

00:05:08.868 --> 00:05:11.200
like my friend Kyle Connaughton
of SingleThread

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:12.834
or Matt Kammerer
of Harbor House.

00:05:12.834 --> 00:05:15.167
These guys are amazing chefs,
but they appreciate

00:05:15.167 --> 00:05:17.367
when somebody takes the time
to cook for them.

00:05:17.367 --> 00:05:19.434
So actually the pressure
is a little bit --

00:05:19.434 --> 00:05:21.167
It comes down a little bit.
-Because no one does it.

00:05:21.167 --> 00:05:22.801
-No. You could serve
them Campbell's soup

00:05:22.801 --> 00:05:25.033
and, like,
a Velveeta grilled cheese,

00:05:25.033 --> 00:05:26.834
and they would
actually be happy.

00:05:26.834 --> 00:05:28.534
-Yeah. They would love it. Yeah.

00:05:28.534 --> 00:05:30.934
You know, I love --
when you first started
coming on our show --

00:05:30.934 --> 00:05:33.234
"How I Built This"
is one of my favorite podcasts.

00:05:33.234 --> 00:05:36.467
It's the greatest. It's so fun.
I love what you do there.

00:05:36.467 --> 00:05:39.334
You basically talk
to different creators

00:05:39.334 --> 00:05:42.167
and you go like, "How did you
start your business?

00:05:42.167 --> 00:05:43.267
How did you fail?"

00:05:43.267 --> 00:05:44.667
Because everyone
has failure stories

00:05:44.667 --> 00:05:47.467
and how to get back up
and survive.

00:05:47.467 --> 00:05:50.501
It's just a fascinating --
it's just -- It's so positive.

00:05:50.501 --> 00:05:53.701
The message is positive.
It's going on season eight.

00:05:53.701 --> 00:05:55.501
-Season eight.
-Congratulations, buddy.

00:05:55.501 --> 00:05:57.434
Are you kidding me?
Season eight?

00:05:57.434 --> 00:05:59.834
-Yeah, we just launched it.
Yeah. Thank you.

00:05:59.834 --> 00:06:03.234
-And "Wow in the World"
is a runaway hit.

00:06:03.234 --> 00:06:04.868
Congratulations on this.
This is giant.

00:06:04.868 --> 00:06:08.334
"Wow in Space."
You can preorder it now.

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:10.601
I know you're talking
to entrepreneurs,

00:06:10.601 --> 00:06:12.300
inventors,
very creative people.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:14.100
I was thinking,
if you don't mind,

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:16.667
I'd like to maybe sell you
some ideas that I have.

00:06:16.667 --> 00:06:18.167
These are major ideas.
-I love it. Yeah.

00:06:18.167 --> 00:06:19.501
-I have to do it
in the right order

00:06:19.501 --> 00:06:21.868
because some are better
than others.

00:06:21.868 --> 00:06:25.534
Alright. Here's one. Alright.

00:06:25.534 --> 00:06:27.801
This one's called
cereal milk boosters.

00:06:27.801 --> 00:06:28.934
-Alright.

00:06:28.934 --> 00:06:30.100
-You could always
change the name.

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:31.734
-Okay. Alright.
-But these are packets

00:06:31.734 --> 00:06:33.634
that are the size of
Sweet'N Lows

00:06:33.634 --> 00:06:35.267
that are used
in, like, boring cereal

00:06:35.267 --> 00:06:38.434
to make them more flavorful
and/or nutritious.

00:06:38.434 --> 00:06:40.601
So you can add probiotics
or electrolytes

00:06:40.601 --> 00:06:42.501
into a regular bowl
of corn flakes.

00:06:42.501 --> 00:06:44.801
You know, just -- You put
the little packet in there.

00:06:44.801 --> 00:06:46.734
Water has all these packets.
Why not milk?

00:06:46.734 --> 00:06:49.434
What happened to milk?
Milk is the new water.

00:06:49.434 --> 00:06:52.067
-Yeah. Right?
-You see what I'm saying?

00:06:52.067 --> 00:06:55.067
Do you see this happening?
You can make chocolate cocoa.

00:06:55.067 --> 00:06:56.734
Why not make it
a little more nutritious?

00:06:56.734 --> 00:06:58.601
-I think --
It's like those packets

00:06:58.601 --> 00:07:00.200
that you add to water
for hydration, right?

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:01.701
You just add it
to your breakfast cereal.

00:07:01.701 --> 00:07:04.033
-Yes.
-There might be something there.

00:07:04.033 --> 00:07:05.300
There might...

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:07.100
-This is a maybe
thumbs-up from...

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:09.133
-I shouldn't have started
with that one

00:07:09.133 --> 00:07:11.601
because that was my worst
one, I think. I thought.

00:07:11.601 --> 00:07:12.968
But alright.
I'll tell you the other one.

00:07:12.968 --> 00:07:16.400
Alright. This one's
called "This Side, That Side."

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:17.834
-Okay.
-Alright.

00:07:17.834 --> 00:07:22.200
It's a whole new
a whole new genre of thing

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:24.067
called jumbotron gaming.

00:07:24.067 --> 00:07:26.100
-Okay.
-Yeah. You ready for this?

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:28.400
So, basically it uses
the volume of one side

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:31.133
of the stadium versus the volume
of the other side of the stadium

00:07:31.133 --> 00:07:32.834
to play a giant two-player game

00:07:32.834 --> 00:07:35.100
on the jumbotron
at stadium events.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.267
-Okay. One side, one side.
I got it.

00:07:37.267 --> 00:07:38.667
-Yeah. So like --
-Jumbotron.

00:07:38.667 --> 00:07:40.234
-Yeah.
Usually there's both teams.

00:07:40.234 --> 00:07:41.901
So it's Kansas City
versus the Jets.

00:07:41.901 --> 00:07:44.400
The Jets fans are on this side.
Kansas City is on that side.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.234
So on the jumbotron,
you see, like, maybe

00:07:46.234 --> 00:07:48.567
a soccer player
about to kick a ball

00:07:48.567 --> 00:07:50.667
and you have 10 seconds
to get as loud as you can.

00:07:50.667 --> 00:07:52.400
-Okay.
-And the power meter goes up.

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:54.167
You build the energy
for the kicker.

00:07:54.167 --> 00:07:55.534
-Yeah.
-Now the other side

00:07:55.534 --> 00:07:57.234
gets 10 seconds
to build the energy

00:07:57.234 --> 00:08:00.400
to block the kick.
-For the goalie. Oh, I see.

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:02.100
-And you play
an actual video game.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:04.000
-In the stadium.
-In the stadium.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.334
While you're waiting...
Yeah.

00:08:05.334 --> 00:08:07.667
-Just, like, during a timeout
or something, yeah.

00:08:07.667 --> 00:08:10.000
-Yeah, a timeout, at halftime,
or whatever it is.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:12.234
Like, those jumbotron games
that everyone's...

00:08:12.234 --> 00:08:13.734
-That seems very doable.

00:08:13.734 --> 00:08:16.801
Like, with the technology
we humans have created now.

00:08:16.801 --> 00:08:18.667
-Two microphones.
-I think we can make this work.

00:08:18.667 --> 00:08:21.901
-Jumbotron gaming
is the new -- is the future.

00:08:21.901 --> 00:08:23.567
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:23.567 --> 00:08:25.100
-I can see that. Okay.
-I can see that one, right?

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:26.334
-Keep them coming.

00:08:26.334 --> 00:08:28.501
-I thought a slap fight
would be funny.

00:08:28.501 --> 00:08:30.100
You know
those cartoon slap fights?

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:34.267
And you just see someone slap
the Kansas City fan -- I just --

00:08:34.267 --> 00:08:35.901
Alright.
So that one gets a thumbs-up,

00:08:35.901 --> 00:08:37.701
I want to say, from you.
-Yeah, I think so.

00:08:37.701 --> 00:08:39.300
-And the other one
got a medium thumb.

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:40.834
Alright.
This is the last one here.

00:08:40.834 --> 00:08:42.234
It's a dog toy.
-Alright. Okay.

00:08:42.234 --> 00:08:44.000
-That you throw
and you play with your --

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:46.033
I'm a proud owner of...
-A dog, too. Yep.

00:08:46.033 --> 00:08:47.567
-I love my dog.

00:08:47.567 --> 00:08:49.133
So you play tug-of-war
with your dog

00:08:49.133 --> 00:08:50.801
you know, with, like,
those chew toys?

00:08:50.801 --> 00:08:52.901
Yeah, those chew-toy things
with two handles on it.

00:08:52.901 --> 00:08:55.000
But the fun catch here
is the toy looks like

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:56.767
two pieces of grilled cheese
split in half.

00:08:56.767 --> 00:08:57.901
And the dog and the owner are

00:08:57.901 --> 00:08:59.634
stretching the cheese
of the sandwich.

